# Project: Jest JavaScript Unit Testing Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or with .test.js/.spec.js suffixes
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest for type-safe test implementations
- Separate unit, integration, and snapshot tests into distinct directories

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices
- Use meaningful test and variable naming
- Implement clean code principles
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach for setup and teardown
- Use beforeAll/afterAll for expensive shared setup (databases, servers)

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state
- Use jest.fn() for mock functions
- Use jest.spyOn() for spying on existing methods
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality over toBe for object comparisons

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for module-level mocking
- Implement jest.spyOn() for method-level spying
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() for time-dependent tests
- Mock HTTP requests with msw (Mock Service Worker) or axios-mock-adapter
- Reset mocks between tests with jest.resetAllMocks() or clearMocks: true in config
- Restore mocks with jest.restoreAllMocks() after each test

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot() for stable UI component output testing
- Use toMatchInlineSnapshot() for small inline snapshots
- Prefer rejects.toThrow() for async error assertions

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with istanbul (default) or v8 provider
- Set meaningful coverage thresholds (statements, branches, functions, lines)
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns for generated or non-testable code
- Generate HTML and lcov reports for local and CI visualization
- Enable collectCoverageFrom to define exact coverage scope

## Performance
- Use --runInBand in CI for sequential execution when workers cause issues
- Enable concurrent test files with --maxWorkers for faster local execution
- Minimize unnecessary setup/teardown operations
- Use --testPathPattern to run specific test files during development

## Snapshot Testing
- Keep snapshots small and focused
- Use toMatchInlineSnapshot() for short, readable inline snapshots
- Commit snapshot files and review them in pull requests
- Update snapshots intentionally with --updateSnapshot flag

## Module Resolution
- Configure moduleNameMapper for path aliases and static assets
- Use transform to handle non-JS files (CSS, images) with identity-obj-proxy
- Configure moduleDirectories for custom module resolution
- Use setupFilesAfterFramework for global test configuration (e.g., Testing Library)

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or fixtures for reusable test data
- Define TypeScript interfaces for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants
- Generate dynamic data with @faker-js/faker when needed

## CI/CD Integration
- Configure --ci flag for strict snapshot behavior in CI
- Use --reporters=jest-junit for pipeline-compatible XML reports
- Set --forceExit if tests hang due to open async handles
- Upload coverage reports to Codecov or SonarQube via lcov output

## Environment Configuration
- Use testEnvironment: 'node' for Node.js and 'jsdom' for browser-like environments
- Configure jest.config.js or jest.config.ts for full TypeScript support
- Use globalSetup and globalTeardown for external service startup/shutdown
- Handle environment variables with dotenv via setupFiles

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure code paths
- Assert on specific error messages and types
- Use expect().rejects for async error testing
- Implement retry logic tests for flaky network operations
- Use --detectOpenHandles to diagnose tests that hang

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with brief inline comments
- Maintain a test helper and mock README
- Include examples for custom matchers (expect.extend)
- Document project-specific mock strategies and module mappings

Remember to keep Jest configuration minimal and explicit, and run tests in watch mode during development with jest --watch.
